# tools/ales_stereo_collapse_silence_witness.rish — stereo_collapse_silence: the interior silence-capper carried into stereo, one joint silence capped in one lockstep, the stripper of ALES148 with its pauses kept (ALES152).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES152.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-055202_fill-ales152-lotus-stereo-collapse-silence.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye (ALES148 — the stereo silence stripper, the zero-cap twin, imported by the selftest to prove the zero-cap law; its silence intersection is the joint-silence this rung caps),
# lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147 — the stereo voice-activity segmenter, run once to read BOTH channels' silences over one shared frame grid, its segment_stereo, StereoSegments, StereoSegmentError reused whole),
# lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 — the mono segmenter and max_segments, through ALES147), lotus/collapse_silence.rye (ALES100 — the mono interior capper, imported by the selftest to prove the lockstep law), lotus/pan.rye
# (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, its cut, its max_clip, the EditError reused whole), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 — the .silent verdict this
# intersects). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, so their silences differ in count and boundary, and
# capping each channel by its own silence would cap different runs to different lengths and tear the stereo image apart.
#
# lotus/stereo_collapse_silence.rye decides ONE cap for both channels — the joint-silence cap: read the jointly-silent frames through ALES148's silence intersection (a frame is dead air only where neither speaker carries
# sound), coalesce consecutive jointly-silent frames into runs, and for each run longer than max_silence cut only the excess (keep the run's first min(count, max_silence) samples) from both channels in lockstep, back to front so
# every earlier run's index stays valid. A frame silent in only one channel is kept whole; only the shared dead air is shortened, and only its excess is cut, so the breath that makes speech legible stays. It validates the shared
# frame length, band, span, and floor once through ALES147's own up-front checks before any cut (BadThreshold on an illegal band, BadRange on a span past the samples, BadFloor on a floor above the peak ceiling, BadFrame on a
# zero/oversized/non-dividing length), so a refusal never caps one channel and leaves the other; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole. The crux is two laws — the zero-cap law (max=0 equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence
# byte for byte on both channels) and the cap law (the result equals a by-hand keep where each jointly-silent run keeps min(count, max_silence) samples and every other frame stays whole).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_collapse_silence_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-collapse-silence: Language EN — stereo_collapse_silence caps every jointly-silent run in both channels of a stereo master to at most max_silence samples, keeping the audio and the shortened joint pauses in order and the stereo image balanced. It segments both channels of the whole clip into frames of one length through ALES147's stereo segmenter, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both channels, coalesces consecutive jointly-silent frames into runs, and for each run longer than max_silence cuts only the excess — keeping the run's first min(count, max_silence) samples — from both channels through ALES2's cut, walking back to front so every earlier run's index stays valid. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; a frame silent in only one channel is kept whole because the other speaker still carries sound, and only shared dead air is shortened. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once before any cut so a refusal never caps one channel and leaves the other."
say "ales-stereo-collapse-silence: Style Radiant — ALES100 caps each silent run of a mono clip to a maximum, keeping the pauses yet shortening the long ones: truncate silence to a quarter-second, the single most-reached-for gesture in every voice-memo cleanup, because it shortens dead air without deleting the breath that makes speech legible. Carried into stereo it meets the wall ALES148 met and no earlier: a StereoClip's defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, yet ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, their silences differing in count and boundary, so capping each channel by its own silence would cap different runs to different lengths and desync the pair, tearing the stereo image apart. This rung cannot run the mono capper twice; it decides one cap that serves both — the joint-silence cap: read the jointly-silent frames through ALES148's own silence intersection, coalesce them into runs, and cut only the excess of the long ones from both channels in lockstep, keeping every frame either speaker fills. A sound panned hard to the left is real audio the right's silence must never shorten. The crux is two laws — the zero-cap law (stereo_collapse_silence at max zero equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both channels, since capping every joint-silent run to zero removes all joint silence, exactly the stereo stripper) and the cap law (the result equals a by-hand keep where each jointly-silent run keeps its first min of count and max_silence samples and every other frame stays whole, per channel, both channels leaving equal length, a cap past the longest joint run leaving the master unchanged, monotone in max). Proven too: a known cap keeping exactly the named samples, the lockstep law (per-channel mono collapse desyncing to lengths ten and fourteen while the stereo capper holds both at fourteen), the degenerate laws (an all-jointly-silent master capping its one coalesced run — empty at zero, unchanged past its length — a no-joint-silent master unchanged on both), and the atomicity laws (an empty clip and a ragged frame length and an inverted band and an illegal floor each refusing by name with both channels untouched and still balanced)."
say "ales-stereo-collapse-silence: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) capped in lockstep by one shared frame length, band, floor, split, and max_silence; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, and all validation runs once up front through ALES147's segment_stereo (ALES92's precheck for band and span, the floor against meter's sample_peak_max, the frame-length divisibility) read-only before a single sample is cut, so a refusal leaves both channels untouched; the forward pass records a bounded jointly-silent frame mask (sized once at max_clip, one bool per frame) by advancing one monotonic cursor per channel across its own tiling run list and marking a frame only where it is silent in both, asserting each cursor names the run that covers the frame start; consecutive marked frames are coalesced into runs and the total excess over max_silence counted so the length postcondition is asserted exactly (both channels lose exactly the joint excess and leave equal length); the backward pass finds each maximal joint run from its last frame, and where its sample count exceeds max_silence cuts the excess from both channels through ALES2's cut with the in-bounds span asserted before each cut so neither can fault and leave the pair half-capped, then steps past the run's start so no run is handled twice; the zero-cap law is proven against ALES148's stereo_trim_silence across six frame lengths, four floors, and three splits, the cap law against an independent by-hand reference (a second implementation reading the frame verdicts by run scan and coalescing) across nine caps, a known cap keeps exactly the named 14 samples per channel, the lockstep law proves the mono capper desyncs to ten and fourteen where this holds both at fourteen, and the degenerate and atomicity cases leave both channels untouched and balanced; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole, siloed to lotus/, edits only by removing whole excess-silent frames, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing, no adaptive floor, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_collapse_silence"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_collapse_silence.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-collapse-silence: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_collapse_silence.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-collapse-silence: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-collapse-silence: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-collapse-silence' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-collapse-silence: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-collapse-silence line"
